    "q-81593": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81593",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 31\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-4)+31\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+31\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
